<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">mid-1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Periplus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea is a Greek guide to navigation and trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, written in the mid-1st century CE. It describes ports and goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It contains the earliest historical mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> and its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoskales">Zoskales</a>, and documents the trade connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> to India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea.jpg" alt="Periplus of the Erythraean Sea" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Periplus_of_the_Erythraean_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A periplus, literally a sailing-around, is a logbook of sailing itineraries with commercial, political and ethnological details of the ports visited. In an era before maps were in general use it served as a combination of atlas and traveller's handbook. The Erythraean Sea meant the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> in the Greek sense, which included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> and, in this work, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> as one maritime area. The text was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> and has been ascribed to dates between the first and third centuries, but a mid-first-century date is now the most commonly accepted. John Hill and L. Casson place it between 40 and 70 CE, and Wilfred Harvey Schoff around 60 CE.</p><p>The author is unknown. Schoff concluded that he was a Greek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and a Roman subject, not a highly educated man, and probably resident at Berenice rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. The tenth-century Byzantine manuscript attributes the work to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, apparently only because it stands beside Arrian's Periplus of the Euxine Sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work has 66 sections, most about the length of a long paragraph, and describes ports and goods from Roman Egyptian harbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-troglodytica">Berenice Troglodytica</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and the Indian coast. At Malao, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a>, it lists imports such as tunics, cups and iron and exports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>. It names Opone as a centre for cinnamon, and Rhapta as the last port of trade on the coast of Azania, whose site is disputed, with candidates on the Rufiji River in Tanzania and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quelimane">Quelimane</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>.</p><p>It records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> was a market for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, whose ruler Zoscales also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adulis">Adulis</a> and Avalites and was acquainted with Greek literature. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> it names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charibael">Charibael</a> as king of the Homerites and Sabaites and a friend of the emperors, and Eleazus, king of the Frankincense Country. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> it describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Barygaza</a>, with its imports of wine and its exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and spikenard, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> and Nelcynda on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>, which traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> with Greek ships. It says a direct sea route across the open ocean to India had been discovered by Hippalus. Its note on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> describes a great inland city, Thina, as a source of raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Periplus survived through a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the fourteenth or fifteenth century in the British Library, a corrupt copy of a tenth-century Byzantine manuscript now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. Sigmund Gelen's edition, published by Hieronymus Froben in 1533, served as the basis of other editions for three centuries. Schoff made the first English translation in 1912. Some names of trade goods occur nowhere else in ancient literature, and the identification of many places remains debated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Periplus of the Erythraean Sea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest historical reference to Aksum as a functioning polity appears in the <b>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</b>, a Greek merchant's guide of the mid-first century CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…long-distance trade: frankincense and myrrh from the south and east, and the transit of Indian and African goods through ports such as Muza and Aden, described in the first-century <b>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</b>, which names Charibael (Karib'il) as king of the Homerites and Sabaeans, ruling from Saphar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</b> records old drachmas of Apollodotus and Menander still current at Barygaza.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…linked Han China, through the Tarim Basin, with the Parthian lands and the Roman Empire, as well as the Indus ports from which ships sailed to the Red Sea; the 1st-century <b>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</b> describes the trade of Barbarikon and Barygaza in goods from the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade networks connected Rome to the Silk Road through Parthian intermediaries and to East Africa and India via the Red Sea ports of Egypt, as documented in the <b>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</b> (1st century CE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</b>, a Greek merchant's guide of the first century CE, describes onyx, muslins and ordinary cloth carried from Paithan and Ter to the great port of Barygaza, modern Bharuch, for export, and Roman coins, amphorae and other imports have been found at Deccan…</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
